{"t":1789630691,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"d45f2103-06ab-4723-8016-177b394d4ebe","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-17T00:54:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 170054\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n854 PM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nThe lingering showers over the Potomac Highlands have now\ndissipated, though additional light showers possible late\ntonight.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Turning warmer and more humid with a return of shower and\n  thunderstorm chances for Thursday and Friday.\n\n- 2) Rain-free and less muggy conditions are likely on Saturday,\n  before the weather turns more unsettled again from Sunday into\n  next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Turning warmer and more humid with a return of \nshower and thunderstorm chances for Thursday and Friday.\n\nThe late afternoon to evening showers and a few rumbles of\nthunder in the Alleghenies and Potomac Highlands have now\ndissipated. Expecting mostly dry conditions for the first half\nof the night, then scattered showers approach the mountains\nlate. Cloud cover is expected to increase after midnight from \nthe north and west.\n\nFor Thursday, the prospects for showers and thunderstorms will\nincrease by mid to late afternoon, especially for the \nAlleghenies, the Shenandoah Valley, and the I-95 corridor. This is\ntied to a slow moving cold front, making a gradual approach from\nthe Ohio Valley and Pennsylvania. As you head farther southeast\nthrough Virginia and into the coastal plain, the chances for\nrain should decrease. Thursday looks like the warmest day of the\nweek, although this is contingent on how much sunshine/heating\ncan take place earlier in the day. At this juncture, we're\nexpecting highs well into the 80s again, with parts of the\nShenandoah Valley up towards the I-95 corridor perhaps making a run\nat 90.\n\nBy Friday, the frontal boundary itself should be across central\nto southern VA. This will lead to a continued chance of showers,\nalong with somewhat cooler readings as compared to Thursday.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain-free and less muggy conditions are likely \non Saturday, before the weather turns more unsettled again from \nSunday into next week.\n\nCurrent indications point to a rain-free start to the weekend,\nwith the above mentioned cold front pushing down into the\nCarolinas, and weak high pressure building down from the north.\n\nHowever, signals also point to more unsettled weather from\nSunday into next week, as a persistent upper ridge axis gets \nshunted towards the deep South, allowing upper-level impulses \nto move across the Mid-Atlantic from the west. This should\nprovide the impetus for at least hit and miss showers and\nthunderstorms on a daily basis.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nPredominantly VFR conds are expected areawide through Thursday\nmorning. Periods of passing showers and thunderstorms are \npossible at times Thursday afternoon and again Thursday evening,\nwith PROB30 groups added to most TAF sites. Could see CIGs \napproach MVFR thresholds, but confidence is low at this time.\n\nA cold front moves through the region early Friday which yields\na shift to northerly winds. Afternoon gusts could approach 15 \nto 20 knots, with any storm chances back toward central \nVirginia.\n\nHigh pressure passing to our north shifts winds out of the east to \nsoutheast Saturday, turning southwesterly by Sunday. Over the \nweekend, gusts up to 15 to perhaps 20 knots are possible. In \ngeneral, expect predominantly VFR conditions to persist, but \nflight conditions could deteriorate as shower and thunderstorm \npotential increase late in the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nCanadian high pressure remains nearby into tonight, favoring\nsub-SCA conditions over the waters.\n\nBy Thursday, this ridge edges offshore ahead of the next cold \nfront. Ahead of this system, southerly winds increase which \ncould near Small Craft Advisory levels on Thursday \nafternoon/evening. This is particularly the case over wider \nwaters of the Chesapeake Bay due to southerly channeling. The \ncold front crosses the waters early Friday which shifts winds \nover to northerly. Breezy conditions in the wake could again \nnear advisory levels. As high pressure tracks across southern \nQuebec, marine winds turn northeasterly into Friday night with \ngusts around 10 to 15 knots.\n\nHigh pressure passing to our north shifts winds out of the east to \nsoutheast Saturday, turning southwesterly on Sunday. Over the \nweekend, gusts up to 15 to perhaps 20 knots are possible.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nAs winds have turned more south to southeasterly, the onshore\nflow has raised tidal anomalies to just below 1 foot. This has\nbeen enough to push a few of the more sensitive locations into \nthe Action stage. Until the cold front moves through early \nFriday, water levels will remain somewhat elevated. Although no\ntidal site is shown to experience any coastal flooding, it will\nneed to be monitored. This is particularly the case at \nAnnapolis, especially during the higher of the two astronomical\nhigh tides.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...BRO/KVH/KRR\nAVIATION...BRO/KVH/KRR\nMARINE...BRO/KVH/KRR\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789630665,"days":{"2026-09-17":{"hi":89,"lo":72,"pop":29,"short":"Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-18":{"hi":86,"lo":66,"pop":3,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-19":{"hi":81,"lo":67,"pop":4,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-20":{"hi":86,"lo":67,"pop":58,"short":"Partly Sunny then Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09-21":{"hi":77,"lo":63,"pop":33,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-22":{"hi":73,"lo":61,"pop":36,"short":"Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"},"2026-09